ed510ea0c1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231895 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231896 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231895 #edb/ref 1061 632546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231895 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "20b0a099-6ca9-5ba0-b3a5-942ce21c5dd3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231895 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231896 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231897 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231896 #edb/ref 1061 632547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231896 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "88098e77-a8d8-51f6-9125-96ff2b4eb4d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231896 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231897 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231897 #edb/ref 1061 632550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231897 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c34b726a-fd07-5d01-a3ce-1006d2aca94b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231897 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231898 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186231899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231898 #edb/ref 1061 632553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231898 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fe5c0cda-3d44-5857-87a2-559959c7d8a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231898 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186231466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231899 #edb/ref 1061 632556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231899 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3617bdf-e6ac-5e17-865e-8d3291845e2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186231899 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186232013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232013 #edb/ref 1064 "We know that the area formula for a triangle is\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$\n\nwhere $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nBut where does this formula come from?\n\nTo understand this, consider the triangle below, with base $b$ and height $h$.\n\n![](images/b0/b0fa27c8446b799668c5493fa2049eb10550134724dc5e54aab8628a33857f0e.png)\n\nLet's draw a rectangle around it, as follows:\n\n![](images/aa/aa8ae2b0c13e0710a8316c920a4d62e0fe822095311d11f4ca3381f022585725.png)\n\nWe have two pairs of right triangles with equal areas (each pair is indicated with arrows).\n\nThe area of the enclosing rectangle is $b \\cdot h$. Since the triangle occupies exactly half of this area, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232013 #edb/ref 1065 14875 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8b463fce-69c3-5e1f-b5c1-15b44aaf6f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232013 #edb/ref 1067 "Proof of the Formula For the Area of a Triangle" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232014 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have considered the area of a *right triangle*.\nBut what is the area of a *general* triangle, one that is not necessarily a right triangle?\n\nIt turns out that the same idea still works.\n\n> A formula for the area of a triangle is\n> $\\textrm{Area} = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot b \\cdot h$,\n> where $b$ is the base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nThis formula works for *any* triangle.\n\nLet's use this formula to find the area of the triangle below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7c8eaf780eaa0559e8ac4dc2557a423b805705bfd3a081635f5284502896168.png)\n\nIn our triangle,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{base} = 7 \\: \\text{ft}, \\qquad \\textrm{height} = 3 \\: \\text{ft}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nArea &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 7 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 21 \\\\\n&= 10.5ft^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the end of this lesson, we'll show how to prove the formula for the area of a triangle.\n\nLet’s see more examples." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232014 #edb/ref 1065 14876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232014 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "35bf275f-a5b4-5e22-8a11-a01a18b1bd80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186232014 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
